1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:02,120
Can an electron pass through a wall?

2
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:05,260
For a carefully defined potential barrier,
yes.

3
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:10,320
Today we will calculate the probability, test
our calculation, and ask what evidence

4
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:11,960
could actually support that conclusion.

5
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:17,840
Imagine a finite potential barrier: a wall in
an energy diagram, rather than an

6
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:18,780
everyday brick wall.

7
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:22,820
Our electron has half an electron volt of
energy.

8
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:27,740
The barrier is one electron volt high and zero
point four five nanometers wide.

9
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:31,680
Classically, the electron cannot reach the
top, so it returns.

10
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:36,720
We use zero potential outside, so the total
energy there is kinetic energy.

11
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:38,980
Pause and predict.

12
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:43,800
In quantum mechanics, must the transmission
probability also be exactly zero?

13
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:46,140
Choose your answer before continuing.

14
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:49,320
Explain which assumption of the classical
picture you are using.

15
00:00:56,387 --> 00:00:59,327
Quantum mechanics describes a state using a
wavefunction.

16
00:00:59,927 --> 00:01:03,847
A finite barrier can support a nonzero
transmitted probability current.

17
00:01:04,427 --> 00:01:08,847
This does not mean the electron secretly
climbs over the barrier, or follows the curve

18
00:01:08,847 --> 00:01:09,427
drawn here.

19
00:01:09,867 --> 00:01:14,947
Detection beyond the barrier is an outcome;
transmission predicts how often we expect

20
00:01:14,947 --> 00:01:15,207
it.

21
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:21,340
The cyan curve is the real part of a
stationary scattering wavefunction, multiplied
by

22
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:22,540
its time dependent phase.

23
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:24,940
Gold shows its probability density.

24
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:27,400
The density is time independent here.

25
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:32,560
A single detection is localized, while these
curves describe the statistics of many

26
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:34,160
identically prepared measurements.

27
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:39,320
Changing the phase changes the real part,
while a stationary probability density stays

28
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:39,640
fixed.

29
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:44,520
On the left, an incoming wave and a reflected
wave overlap.

30
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:46,920
Their interference makes the density ripple.

31
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:49,860
On the right, only an outgoing wave is
present.

32
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:55,180
Our incident amplitude is one, so these
scattering states are not normalized to one

33
00:01:55,180 --> 00:01:56,020
over all space.

34
00:01:56,620 --> 00:01:59,300
Transmission is a ratio of probability
currents.

35
00:01:59,940 --> 00:02:04,220
Interference changes the density on the left,
while the net probability current remains

36
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:04,720
constant.

37
00:02:06,220 --> 00:02:07,220
Pause and predict.

38
00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:11,420
At each edge of this finite barrier, which
quantities must be continuous?

39
00:02:11,940 --> 00:02:12,900
The wavefunction alone?

40
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:14,960
The wavefunction and its spatial derivative?

41
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:16,440
Or the potential itself?

42
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:20,500
Our model has a constant electron mass and no
delta function potential.

43
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:22,910
Keep those assumptions in your reasoning.

44
00:02:31,220 --> 00:02:35,560
Both the wavefunction and its first spatial
derivative are continuous at each edge.

45
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:39,360
There are four matching equations for four
unknown amplitudes.

46
00:02:39,960 --> 00:02:41,300
The potential may jump.

47
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:46,080
For interfaces described by different
effective masses, the matching law needs a

48
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:50,240
suitable effective mass model; do not copy
these conditions without checking its

49
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:50,660
assumptions.

50
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:55,600
Integrating the equation across a shrinking
interval gives the derivative condition for

51
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:56,420
a finite step.

52
00:02:57,553 --> 00:03:01,933
The time independent Schrödinger equation
relates the curvature of the wavefunction to

53
00:03:01,933 --> 00:03:04,333
the difference between potential and total
energy.

54
00:03:05,133 --> 00:03:08,553
Here, h-bar is Planck's constant divided by
two pi.

55
00:03:09,213 --> 00:03:11,613
Outside the barrier the solutions oscillate.

56
00:03:12,013 --> 00:03:16,753
Inside, where the energy is lower than the
potential, the solutions are exponentials.

57
00:03:17,573 --> 00:03:20,573
The decay constant kappa has units of inverse
length.

58
00:03:21,553 --> 00:03:25,453
Notice how the sign of the curvature
distinguishes the oscillatory and exponential

59
00:03:25,453 --> 00:03:25,933
regions.

60
00:03:27,320 --> 00:03:32,820
A finite barrier requires a combination of two
exponential solutions, not just one

61
00:03:32,820 --> 00:03:33,940
decaying exponential.

62
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:37,440
Matching the two interfaces produces the
transmitted wave.

63
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:40,720
In this stationary model total energy is
conserved.

64
00:03:41,090 --> 00:03:45,990
The electron does not borrow energy, and the
uncertainty principle is not a license to

65
00:03:45,990 --> 00:03:47,350
violate energy conservation.

66
00:03:48,430 --> 00:03:52,790
The complex coefficients of those exponentials
together allow a conserved current

67
00:03:52,790 --> 00:03:53,630
inside the barrier.

68
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:59,735
Suppose the calculated transmission
coefficient is about zero point one four.

69
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,080
Does that mean every electron loses fourteen
percent of its energy?

70
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:07,220
Or that around fourteen percent of a large
incident ensemble is transmitted?

71
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:10,700
Predict an outcome for ten thousand
independent electrons.

72
00:04:11,290 --> 00:04:14,090
Include the fact that a finite count will
fluctuate.

73
00:04:22,320 --> 00:04:26,480
The exact expression on screen gives about
zero point one four for our default

74
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:27,000
parameters.

75
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:31,820
Roughly fourteen hundred of ten thousand
incident electrons are expected to transmit,

76
00:04:31,820 --> 00:04:33,500
with statistical fluctuations.

77
00:04:34,220 --> 00:04:39,040
Equal potentials and equal masses on both
sides mean the transmitted amplitude squared

78
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:40,160
equals transmission.

79
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:43,880
Reflected and transmitted currents add to the
incident current.

80
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:46,780
An individual trial gives one detection
outcome.

81
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:50,480
Repeating the preparation makes the
probability prediction testable.

82
00:04:51,587 --> 00:04:54,867
Now hold energy and barrier height fixed while
changing width.

83
00:04:55,667 --> 00:04:58,887
Notice how the outgoing amplitude shrinks as
the barrier thickens.

84
00:04:59,347 --> 00:05:03,047
The wavefunction is calculated by matching
both interfaces each time.

85
00:05:03,527 --> 00:05:08,687
The animation changes the model between
stationary solutions; it is not a simulation
of

86
00:05:08,687 --> 00:05:10,807
a moving wall or an electron trajectory.

87
00:05:11,567 --> 00:05:15,957
At a quarter nanometer, transmission is about
forty eight percent; at zero point six

88
00:05:15,957 --> 00:05:17,927
five, about three and a half percent.

89
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:23,720
When kappa times width is large, transmission
falls approximately exponentially with

90
00:05:23,720 --> 00:05:24,020
width.

91
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:26,920
The prefactor still depends on energy and
height.

92
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:30,360
Use the exact expression when the barrier is
not opaque.

93
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:36,160
Taking a logarithm turns the approximate
exponential trend into a line, which is useful

94
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:37,320
when designing measurements.

95
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:42,500
The factor of two in the exponent comes from
squaring the magnitude of an amplitude.

96
00:05:44,120 --> 00:05:45,120
Pause and predict.

97
00:05:45,780 --> 00:05:49,860
At fixed energy and height, does doubling
barrier width simply cut transmission in

98
00:05:49,860 --> 00:05:50,100
half?

99
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:52,920
Or can it reduce transmission much more
strongly?

100
00:05:53,700 --> 00:05:56,500
Write the ratio you would calculate using the
exact formula.

101
00:05:57,040 --> 00:06:01,100
Do not assume the exponential approximation is
exact for the thinner barrier.

102
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:14,300
For widths zero point four five and zero point
nine nanometers, transmission drops from

103
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:17,400
about fourteen percent to about zero point six
percent.

104
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:22,260
In the laboratory, compare the closed form
with independent differential equation

105
00:06:22,260 --> 00:06:22,760
integration.

106
00:06:23,420 --> 00:06:26,420
Also check continuity and constant probability
current.

107
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:31,780
Defining reflection as one minus transmission
alone would not independently test

108
00:06:31,780 --> 00:06:32,420
conservation.

109
00:06:33,300 --> 00:06:35,840
Two implementations can share the same unit
error.

110
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:39,280
That is why dimensions and limiting cases also
matter.

111
00:06:40,453 --> 00:06:44,633
Raising energy toward the barrier height
increases transmission in this below barrier

112
00:06:44,633 --> 00:06:45,053
model.

113
00:06:45,393 --> 00:06:48,653
But do not extrapolate this equation past its
stated domain.

114
00:06:49,273 --> 00:06:53,453
At or above the barrier, use the correct
limiting expression or the oscillatory

115
00:06:53,453 --> 00:06:53,873
solution.

116
00:06:54,463 --> 00:06:59,463
As the energy gap shrinks, the decay length
grows, allowing more amplitude to reach the

117
00:06:59,463 --> 00:06:59,763
exit.

118
00:07:00,953 --> 00:07:05,893
A higher barrier increases the decay constant
and reduces transmission for these fixed

119
00:07:05,893 --> 00:07:06,413
parameters.

120
00:07:07,373 --> 00:07:09,993
Width, height, and effective mass all matter.

121
00:07:10,713 --> 00:07:14,933
In an actual device, voltage can change the
barrier shape as well as the occupied

122
00:07:14,933 --> 00:07:15,773
electron energies.

123
00:07:16,403 --> 00:07:21,203
Always convert the energy and length units
consistently before evaluating an

124
00:07:21,203 --> 00:07:21,663
exponential.

125
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:23,880
Pause and predict.

126
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:27,620
Does tunneling require the electron to borrow
extra energy?

127
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:32,700
Check your answer against our time independent
equation, which has one fixed total

128
00:07:32,700 --> 00:07:34,000
energy on both sides.

129
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:37,900
The wave inside the barrier does not describe
a classical trajectory.

130
00:07:45,020 --> 00:07:46,740
No energy loan is required.

131
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:52,620
For our static, elastic, lossless barrier,
transmitted and reflected electrons have the

132
00:07:52,620 --> 00:07:55,220
same total energy as the incident electrons.

133
00:07:55,980 --> 00:07:59,040
Interactions that exchange energy require a
richer model.

134
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:04,080
The decaying amplitude is compatible with one
conserved energy throughout this elastic

135
00:08:04,080 --> 00:08:05,040
scattering problem.

136
00:08:06,187 --> 00:08:10,927
In scanning tunneling microscopy, a conducting
tip approaches a conducting sample.

137
00:08:11,567 --> 00:08:15,207
A small bias allows a net tunneling current
across the vacuum gap.

138
00:08:15,687 --> 00:08:20,767
Because transmission is sensitive to
separation, a feedback loop can adjust tip
height

139
00:08:20,767 --> 00:08:24,587
to maintain a chosen current as the tip scans
across the surface.

140
00:08:25,427 --> 00:08:29,247
The tip can carry a current without touching
the sample: that is the practical

141
00:08:29,247 --> 00:08:29,767
opportunity.

142
00:08:33,187 --> 00:08:37,067
The resulting image is not simply a photograph
of hard atomic surfaces.

143
00:08:37,727 --> 00:08:42,347
The current depends on electronic states,
bias, and tip properties as well as

144
00:08:42,347 --> 00:08:42,707
separation.

145
00:08:43,397 --> 00:08:47,877
Our rectangular barrier captures a useful
sensitivity, but a realistic current

146
00:08:47,877 --> 00:08:52,817
calculation must include occupied states,
empty states, and the energy window selected

147
00:08:52,817 --> 00:08:53,477
by the bias.

148
00:08:54,197 --> 00:08:58,477
An apparent hill in a current map can
therefore reflect electronic structure as well
as

149
00:08:58,477 --> 00:08:58,777
geometry.

150
00:09:00,087 --> 00:09:01,127
Pause and predict.

151
00:09:01,847 --> 00:09:05,547
If the current doubles in your measurement,
has the surface height necessarily doubled?

152
00:09:06,167 --> 00:09:10,647
Consider a different tip, a change of local
electronic states, and a different bias.

153
00:09:11,027 --> 00:09:15,167
Which controlled comparisons would help
distinguish geometry from electronic contrast?

154
00:09:15,797 --> 00:09:18,217
Write a prediction before you reveal the
feedback.

155
00:09:26,087 --> 00:09:28,687
No simple doubling rule connects current and
height.

156
00:09:29,447 --> 00:09:34,107
Semiconductor oxides and organic electronic
interfaces also have complicated transport:

157
00:09:34,887 --> 00:09:39,267
direct tunneling may compete with thermally
activated injection, hopping, and trap

158
00:09:39,267 --> 00:09:40,167
assisted processes.

159
00:09:40,847 --> 00:09:46,167
Effective masses, nonrectangular barriers,
inelastic scattering, and distributions of

160
00:09:46,167 --> 00:09:48,087
molecular arrangements can all matter.

161
00:09:48,967 --> 00:09:53,467
A fitted barrier is a model parameter, not
automatically a measured material constant.

162
00:09:54,017 --> 00:09:58,957
In an organic layer, molecular orientation and
disorder can also change the available

163
00:09:58,957 --> 00:09:59,417
pathways.

164
00:10:00,720 --> 00:10:05,120
A small current through a nominally insulating
layer is evidence of transport, but it

165
00:10:05,120 --> 00:10:07,160
does not identify a unique mechanism.

166
00:10:08,020 --> 00:10:10,440
Measure a thickness series and a temperature
series.

167
00:10:11,280 --> 00:10:14,160
Control contact area, defects, and instrument
leakage.

168
00:10:14,750 --> 00:10:19,530
Compare multiple plausible models and examine
their residuals, rather than judging only

169
00:10:19,530 --> 00:10:21,210
whether one curve looks convincing.

170
00:10:22,250 --> 00:10:25,610
Choose measurements where the competing
mechanisms make different, observable

171
00:10:25,610 --> 00:10:26,170
predictions.

172
00:10:27,520 --> 00:10:28,600
Pause and predict.

173
00:10:29,320 --> 00:10:32,240
An exponential decrease of current with
thickness is observed.

174
00:10:32,820 --> 00:10:35,260
Does that conclusively prove direct tunneling?

175
00:10:35,860 --> 00:10:40,040
Consider whether defects, thickness dependent
morphology, or another transport

176
00:10:40,040 --> 00:10:41,980
mechanism could generate a similar trend.

177
00:10:42,500 --> 00:10:47,020
State one additional measurement and explain
what competing predictions it would test.

178
00:10:54,087 --> 00:10:58,307
The trend supports a tunneling explanation,
but is not conclusive by itself.

179
00:10:59,007 --> 00:11:02,807
Weak temperature dependence under controlled
conditions can strengthen the case for

180
00:11:02,807 --> 00:11:07,387
direct elastic tunneling, while thermally
activated behavior motivates alternatives.

181
00:11:07,987 --> 00:11:09,647
No single signature is universal.

182
00:11:10,267 --> 00:11:14,987
Report parameter uncertainty, measurement
limits, model assumptions, and what

183
00:11:14,987 --> 00:11:17,087
observation would make you change your
explanation.

184
00:11:18,187 --> 00:11:22,447
Confidence grows from consistent evidence
across conditions, rather than from one

185
00:11:22,447 --> 00:11:23,247
successful fit.

186
00:11:24,420 --> 00:11:25,860
Open the companion laboratory.

187
00:11:26,680 --> 00:11:31,140
Predict first, change the parameters, and
compare analytical and numerical results.

188
00:11:31,780 --> 00:11:34,360
Then design an experiment for an unfamiliar
interface.

189
00:11:34,980 --> 00:11:38,960
Submit your own explanations, code,
uncertainty analysis, and reflection.

190
00:11:39,580 --> 00:11:42,720
Completing a quiz is practice, not proof of
understanding.

191
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:47,720
The goal is to make a defensible scientific
judgment and know where your model ends.

192
00:11:48,360 --> 00:11:52,400
Connect a physical assumption, a numerical
check, and a measurement that could change

193
00:11:52,400 --> 00:11:52,960
your conclusion.

